---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Demo from '../components/Demo.astro';
import { Button } from '@era/components/button/Button';
import { Badge } from '@era/components/badge/Badge';
import { Input } from '@era/components/input/Input';
import { Select } from '@era/components/select/Select';
import { Alert } from '@era/components/alert/Alert';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const VENDORS = [
  { value: 'northwind', label: 'Northwind Supply', hint: '4 invoices this quarter' },
  { value: 'calder', label: 'Calder Logistics', hint: '2 invoices this quarter' },
  { value: 'brightwell', label: 'Brightwell Print', hint: 'New vendor, first payment held' },
];
---

<Base title="Overview" description="ERA is a multi-brand design system: three brands and two modes from one set of components.">
  <h1>ERA</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A multi-brand design system. Three brands and two modes come out of one set of components, and no component knows which
    brand or mode it is in.
  </p>

  <p>
    Use the swatches at the top of this page to change brand, and the Light and Dark button to change mode. Everything on
    every page here follows, including this site's own chrome, because the docs are built with the system they document.
  </p>

  <Demo label="The same components, whichever brand and mode you have selected">
    <Button client:load>Release payment</Button>
    <Button intent="outline" client:load>Hold for review</Button>
    <Button intent="danger" client:load>Reject</Button>
    <Badge tone="success" fill="subtle" dot>Released</Badge>
    <Badge tone="warning" fill="subtle" dot>Held</Badge>
  </Demo>

  <h2>What it actually is</h2>

  <p>
    ERA started as an answer to a problem that shows up in every company running more than one product: three products, three
    sets of buttons, three definitions of "error red", and a redesign that has to be done three times. The usual fix is to
    make one component library and let each product override it, which lasts about a quarter before the overrides become the
    real system.
  </p>

  <p>
    The approach here is to put the variation in the tokens rather than in the components. There are three layers, and each
    one is allowed to know about exactly one thing below it.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it holds</th><th>What changes it</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Primitives</strong></td>
        <td>Raw values with no opinion: 11-step ramps, a 4px spacing scale, type sizes, radius steps, stroke weights.</td>
        <td>Nothing. These are constants.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Brand</strong></td>
        <td>Which primitive each brand reaches for, plus radius roles so a brand can shape buttons differently from cards.</td>
        <td><code>data-brand</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Semantics</strong></td>
        <td>Names for jobs, not for colours: <code>--sem-status-error-bg</code> rather than <code>--red-700</code></td>
        <td><code>data-theme</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Components read the semantic layer and nothing else. That is the rule the whole thing rests on, and it is what lets a
    brand swap be two attributes:
  </p>

  <pre><code>&lt;html data-brand="brand-2" data-theme="dark"&gt;</code></pre>

  <h2>What that buys</h2>

  <p>
    A form is the honest test, because forms are where systems usually leak. Nothing below is brand-aware or mode-aware.
    Change either one at the top of this page and read it again.
  </p>

  <Demo label="One form, three brands, two modes, no overrides">
    <div style="display: grid; gap: var(--spacing-space-16); max-width: 380px; width: 100%;">
      <Input label="Invoice number" defaultValue="INV-2301" client:load />
      <Select label="Vendor" options={VENDORS} defaultValue="calder" client:load />
      <Alert
        tone="warning"
        title="This invoice is a near-duplicate."
        description="INV-2301 from the same vendor was paid on 11 Oct for the same amount."
        client:load
      />
      <div style="display: flex; gap: var(--spacing-space-8);">
        <Button client:load>Release</Button>
        <Button intent="ghost" client:load>Cancel</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Demo>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><a href={`${base}/using-era`}>Using ERA</a> is the honest answer to "can I use this in my project", including what does not work yet.</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/tokens`}>How tokens work</a> goes through the three layers properly, with the naming rules.</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/accessibility`}>Accessibility</a> covers the contrast audit, what it caught, and the one exemption.</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/storybook/`}>The Storybook</a> has every component, every state, and a live contrast audit you can run per brand.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Built by</h2>

  <p>
    Sanyam Sharma, a product designer who works on design systems. ERA is a personal project, not a company system, and the
    code and the Figma library are both open. If something here is wrong or unclear,
    <a href="https://github.com/DARKHACK4949/Era-Design-Foundation-Story-book/issues">open an issue</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
